    "text": "\n000\nFXUS65 KVEF 112144\nAFDVEF\n\nArea Forecast Discussion\nNational Weather Service Las Vegas NV\n244 PM PDT Sun Oct 11 2026\n\n.KEY MESSAGES...\n\n* Widespread scattered shower activity will continue through Monday, \n  with elevated flash flood risk continuing for Clark, Lincoln, and \n  Mohave counties through tonight.\n\n* Gusty south winds continue through the day today, gradually \n  tapering off through Monday.\n\n* Below-normal temperatures expected, with portions of the \n  southwestern Great Basin freezing early in the work week.\n\n&&\n\n\n.DISCUSSION...Today through Saturday.\n\nWidespread heavy rainfall this morning resulted in flood impacts \nacross portions of southeastern California, southern Nevada, and \nnorthwestern Arizona with peak rainfall measurements ranging from \n2.50 to over 3.00 inches. The center of the remnants of Rachel will \ncontinue to push north-northeastward through the day today, bringing \npersistent concerns for flash flooding across Clark County, Lincoln \nCounty, and Mohave County through late tonight. Meanwhile, Inyo, San \nBernardino, Esmeralda, and central Nye counties have been removed \nfrom the Flood Watch. While isolated-to-scattered showers remain \npossible in these areas, the widespread concern for flash flooding \nhas subsided. A longwave trough will approach the region Monday, \nwith its associated trough axis swinging through the Desert \nSouthwest Monday night. As a result, a resurgence of precipitation \nchances return Monday evening, though rain rates are expected to be \nlow-to-moderate. Chances of rain will decrease through the day on \nTuesday.\n\nGusty south winds blew through the region this morning, with several \nsites across Mohave and Lincoln counties observing 55-65 mph winds \nassociated with the main circulation of the remnants of Rachel. Wind \ngust speeds have decreased through the day today, so the High Wind \nWarning has been downgraded to a Wind Advisory through 5 AM Monday \nmorning. Expect persistent southerly winds across the forecast area \nbetween 30 and 45 mph today. On Monday, gusts will continue, but \nwill range between 20 and 30 mph.\n\nA cold front associated with the aforementioned upper-level trough \nwill push through the Desert Southwest Monday night, bringing much \ncooler temperatures to the region. A Freeze Watch is in effect for \nthe northern Owens Valley, Esmeralda County, and central Nye County, \ndue to the expectation of the first freeze of the season Tuesday \nmorning. For the remainder of the region, temperatures are expected \nto remain above-freezing, but will range 15-20 degrees below-\nnormal... equating to forecast high temperatures in Las Vegas in the \nupper-60s on Tuesday. Temperatures gradually recover through the \nwork week into next weekend as a heights rise in the Desert \nSouthwest.\n&&\n\n\n.AVIATION...For Harry Reid...For the 18Z Forecast Package... \nAnother challenging forecast period ahead, with periodic showers \nexpected through mid-evening, with a brief window late afternoon \nthrough sunset when there is around a 20% chance of thunderstorms \nto develop. Confidence in the occurrence of thunderstorms is low \nto moderate, however, showers and storms will be capable of \nproducing brief moderate to heavy rain, resulting in ceilings down\nto around 3kft and visibility below 6SM. This evening, conditions\nwill slowly improve as showers diminish and shift northeast of \nthe Las Vegas Valley, though ceilings will remain SCT- BKN between\n7-10kft. South-southwesterly wind gusts to 25-35KT will continue \nthrough late evening outside of convective influences, with gusts \ndiminishing thereafter. Mid-morning, have reintroduced VCSH for \nadditional development, with a return of gusty southwesterly winds\nexpected early afternoon. \n\nFor the rest of southern Nevada, northwest Arizona and southeast\nCalifornia...For the 18Z Forecast Package...Unsettled weather\ncontinues across the region, with periodic showers and \nthunderstorms expected across southern Nevada, northwestern \nArizona, and portions of southeastern California through late \nevening. The greatest likelihood for isolated thunderstorms and \nheavier rain from showers exists across southern Nevada (KLAS, \nKVGT, KHND), northwestern Arizona, and the Lower Colorado River \nValley (KIFP and KEED), with showers expected to taper off from \nsouthwest to northeast this evening into tonight. Additional \nshowers and isolated thunderstorms will develop late tonight \nacross southern areas, and progress northward through Monday. \nCeilings in the vicinity of showers and storms could drop to 3kft \nor less, with improvement to 7-12kft expected tonight. \nElevated/gusty winds will continue, with gusts to 25-35KT common \nacross the region through this evening. Gusts diminish overnight, \nthen return for most locations near or just beyond the end of the \nforecast period.\n&&\n\n.SPOTTER INFORMATION STATEMENT...Spotters are encouraged to report\nany significant weather or impacts according to standard operating\nprocedures.\n\n&&\n\n$$\n\nDISCUSSION...Soulat\nAVIATION...Phillipson\n\nFor more forecast information...see us on our webpage:\nhttps://weather.gov/lasvegas or follow us on Facebook and Twitter\n",
